    caseStudy: { problem: "The bookkeeper's weekly two hours of manual data copying between three separate tools was repetitive, error-prone, and directly limited her ability to take real time off.", decision: "Rather than automating everything at once, she started with the single most repetitive, clearly-defined task: syncing new client data from her invoicing tool into her follow-up spreadsheet.", action: "She set up a narrow, specific automation for that one task, with a simple weekly review step to catch anything unusual, and only expanded to a second automation once the first had run reliably for a month.", result: "Her weekly two-hour task became a fifteen-minute review, and the reliability of the narrow automation gave her enough confidence in the system to take a full week off for the first time in three years.", lesson: "Starting narrow and specific, rather than attempting to automate everything at once, was what made the system trustworthy enough to actually rely on." },
